```

## **Best Practices**

### ✅ DO:
- **Test behavior, not implementation** - focus on what the user sees
- **Use semantic queries** - `getByRole`, `getByLabelText`, `getByText`
- **One assertion per test** - each test should verify one behavior
- **Descriptive test names** - clearly describe what you're testing
- **Test grouping** - use `describe` for logical grouping
- **Cleanup after tests** - use `afterEach` to reset state
- **Mock external dependencies** - isolate components from external services

### ❌ DON'T:
- Don't test implementation details (internal state, private methods)
- Don't rely on specific DOM structure
- Don't use `container.querySelector` instead of semantic queries
- Don't make tests dependent on each other
- Don't test library code (React, Material-UI)
- **Don't write time-dependent unstable tests**: `setTimeout`, current time dependency without `jest.useFakeTimers()`/time mocking
- **Don't hide logic in beforeEach**: complex Arrange parts in hooks make tests unclear
- **Don't write one test for multiple scenarios**: split into separate cases
- **Don't test implementation details**: test user-visible behavior, not internal state (especially for React components)
- **Don't use React Testing Library queries incorrectly**: use `screen.getByRole`, `getByText` instead of `querySelector`

## **Avoiding React Component Rendering Issues**

### Common Pitfalls and Solutions

When writing unit tests for React components, avoid these common mistakes that lead to console errors and warnings:

#### 1. **setState on Unmounted Components**
**❌ DON'T: Create component instances without rendering**
```typescript
// BAD: Creates component instance without React rendering
test('bad test', () => {
  const component = new MyComponent({ prop: 'value' });
  component.handleChange(); // Warning: Can't call setState on a component that is not yet mounted
});
```

**✅ DO: Always render components or test through UI**
```typescript
// GOOD: Test through UI interaction
test('handles user input correctly', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  const mockOnChange = jest.fn();

  render(<MyComponent onChange={mockOnChange} />);

  const input = screen.getByRole('textbox');
  await user.type(input, 'test value');

  expect(mockOnChange).toHaveBeenCalledWith('test value');
});

// GOOD: If testing methods directly, set state manually without setState
test('validates input correctly', () => {
  const component = new MyComponent({ onChange: jest.fn() });

  // Set state directly without calling setState
  Object.defineProperty(component, 'state', {
    value: { value: 'test' },
    writable: true
  });

  const result = component.validateInput();
  expect(result).toBe(true);
});
```

#### 2. **TypeError with Undefined Props**
**❌ DON'T: Pass undefined props without handling**
```typescript
// Component code
class MyComponent extends React.Component<{ items?: string[] }> {
  render() {
    return <div>{this.props.items.map(item => <span>{item}</span>)}</div>; // TypeError if items is undefined
  }
}

// Test code
test('bad test', () => {
  render(<MyComponent items={undefined} />); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')
});
```

**✅ DO: Handle undefined props in components**
```typescript
// Component code
class MyComponent extends React.Component<{ items?: string[] }> {
  render() {
    const { items = [] } = this.props; // Default to empty array
    return <div>{items.map(item => <span key={item}>{item}</span>)}</div>;
  }
}

// Or check explicitly
class MyComponent extends React.Component<{ items?: string[] }> {
  render() {
    const items = this.props.items || []; // Fallback to empty array
    return <div>{items.map(item => <span key={item}>{item}</span>)}</div>;
  }
}

// Test code
test('handles undefined items gracefully', () => {
  render(<MyComponent items={undefined} />);

  expect(screen.getByRole('generic')).toBeInTheDocument(); // No error thrown
  expect(screen.queryByRole('listitem')).not.toBeInTheDocument(); // Empty list
});
```

#### 3. **Incomplete Material-UI Component Mocks**
**❌ DON'T: Mock MUI components without handling all props**
```typescript
// BAD: Mock doesn't handle specific props
jest.mock('@mui/material/Autocomplete', () => {
  return function MockAutocomplete({ options, value, renderInput }) {
    return (
      <div>
        {renderInput({ params: {} })}
        {/* Missing: isOptionEqualToValue, filterSelectedOptions, noOptionsText, etc. */}
      </div>
    );
  };
});

// Results in React warnings: "React does not recognize the `isOptionEqualToValue` prop"
```

**✅ DO: Mock all props used by components**
```typescript
// GOOD: Handle all props to avoid DOM attribute warnings
jest.mock('@mui/material/Autocomplete', () => {
  return function MockAutocomplete({
    options,
    value,
    renderInput,
    getOptionLabel,
    isOptionEqualToValue,    // Accept but don't use in mock
    filterSelectedOptions,   // Accept but don't use in mock
    noOptionsText,           // Accept and use in mock
    onChange,
    multiple,
    id,
    ...props                 // Spread remaining props
  }: any) {
    return (
      <div data-testid="autocomplete" {...props}>
        {renderInput({ params: { InputProps: {}, inputProps: {} } })}
        <div data-testid="options">
          {options?.map((option: any, index: number) => (
            <div
              key={option.id}
              data-testid={`option-${index}`}
              onClick={() => onChange?.(null, multiple ? [option] : option)}
            >
              {getOptionLabel?.(option) || option}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        {noOptionsText && options?.length === 0 && (
          <div data-testid="no-options">{noOptionsText}</div>
        )}
      </div>
    );
  };
});
```

#### 4. **Testing Internal Component Methods**
**❌ DON'T: Test private methods directly**
```typescript
// BAD: Testing implementation details
test('internal validation logic', () => {
  const component = new MyComponent({});
  const result = component.validateInput('test'); // Testing private method
  expect(result).toBe(true);
});
```

**✅ DO: Test behavior through public interface**
```typescript
// GOOD: Test what user sees and interacts with
test('shows validation error for invalid input', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<MyForm />);

  const input = screen.getByRole('textbox');
  const submitButton = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i });

  await user.type(input, 'invalid-input');
  await user.click(submitButton);

  expect(screen.getByText('Invalid input')).toBeInTheDocument();
  expect(screen.getByRole('textbox')).toHaveAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
});
```

## **Element Queries (Priority)**

1. **getByRole** - most accessible approach
2. **getByLabelText** - for forms
3. **getByPlaceholderText** - for input fields
4. **getByText** - for text content
5. **getByDisplayValue** - for form elements with values
6. **getByAltText** - for images
7. **getByTitle** - for elements with title
8. **getByTestId** - last resort

```typescript
// ✅ Good
screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i });
screen.getByLabelText(/username/i);

// ❌ Bad
container.querySelector('.submit-button');
screen.getByTestId('submit-button'); // only if no other options
```

